		{
			"id": "zKsOZOPs",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 1775.1016266075726,
			"y": 1867.4982193721155,
			"width": 934.9989013671875,
			"height": 250,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b2X",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 2015446660,
			"version": 734,
			"versionNonce": 981703356,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783545997962,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Definition: Each node can have at most two children (Left, Right).\nConstraint: Strictly limited to 2 children. \n            This simplicity is why it is the most researched tree structure.\nKey Examples:\n  - Binary Search Tree (BST): Enforces order (Left < Root < Right) to enable O(log N)\n    searching.\n  - Balanced Binary Tree (AVL, Red-Black): Uses rotation algorithms to ensure the \n    tree height stays O(log N), preventing the \"linked-list\" performance trap.\n  - Binary Indexed Tree (Fenwick Tree): A specialized structure using a binary tree \n    layout to calculate prefix sums in an array in O(log N) time.",
			"rawText": "Definition: Each node can have at most two children (Left, Right).\nConstraint: Strictly limited to 2 children. \n            This simplicity is why it is the most researched tree structure.\nKey Examples:\n  - Binary Search Tree (BST): Enforces order (Left < Root < Right) to enable O(log N) \n    searching.\n  - Balanced Binary Tree (AVL, Red-Black): Uses rotation algorithms to ensure the \n    tree height stays O(log N), preventing the \"linked-list\" performance trap.\n  - Binary Indexed Tree (Fenwick Tree): A specialized structure using a binary tree \n    layout to calculate prefix sums in an array in O(log N) time.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": "1S4YyxyjRy3wl4OS6BjaV",
			"originalText": "Definition: Each node can have at most two children (Left, Right).\nConstraint: Strictly limited to 2 children. \n            This simplicity is why it is the most researched tree structure.\nKey Examples:\n  - Binary Search Tree (BST): Enforces order (Left < Root < Right) to enable O(log N) \n    searching.\n  - Balanced Binary Tree (AVL, Red-Black): Uses rotation algorithms to ensure the \n    tree height stays O(log N), preventing the \"linked-list\" performance trap.\n  - Binary Indexed Tree (Fenwick Tree): A specialized structure using a binary tree \n    layout to calculate prefix sums in an array in O(log N) time.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
